usb: dwc2: verify the bus before raising the root port back to high speed

The adaptive root-port speed added in 03c43ac drops to full speed correctly,
but the way back up was optimistic and flapped the port, which is what a user
feels as "I plugged a slow device in next to a fast one and the keyboard went
bad".

_dwc2_hcd_endpoint_disable() fires on EVERY endpoint teardown - a driver
unbinding, a device being reconfigured, an unrelated device being unplugged -
and dwc2_speed_note_endpoint_gone() treated that as proof the bus was clear:
it set split_devices = false and asked for high speed. With a full-speed
keyboard still attached the port bounced up, tore down every device on the
bus, took the keyboard's first URB, found it needed a split after all, and
bounced straight back down. Each leg powers the port off for 200 ms and
re-enumerates the whole tree.

Reproduced deliberately on the board with an UNRELATED device - "echo 0 >
1-1.3/authorized; echo 1 > 1-1.3/authorized" - while a full-speed hub, mouse
and keyboard were attached:

    [ 331.054] root port -> high speed (trying: nothing left that needs splits)
    [ 331.280] usb 1-1: USB disconnect, device number 7
    [ 331.281] usb 1-1.3: USB disconnect, device number 8
    [ 331.511] usb 1-1.4: USB disconnect, device number 9
    [ 332.992] device 0 needs split transactions: dropping to full speed
    [ 332.994] root port -> full speed
    [ 333.032] usb 1-1: USB disconnect, device number 10
    [ 334.979] usb 1-1.4: new full-speed USB device number 17

Two extra speed switches and four disconnects: ~4 s with no keyboard and no
mouse, from an event that had nothing to do with the device needing splits.

So ask the bus instead of guessing. Before raising the speed, walk the device
tree and look for a device that is slower than high speed AND behind a hub -
parent->parent is the root hub, so a full-speed device plugged straight into
the root port has no grandparent and needs no split, the port simply runs at
its speed. If one is found, decline and leave the port alone; declining costs
nothing and disturbs no device.

The walk takes a mutex, so it lives in the delayed work, which runs in process
context holding neither hsotg->lock nor any device lock. It is also the
cheapest place: dwc2_speed_note_endpoint_gone() already returns immediately
unless the port is at full speed, ignores anything within 10 s of a switch,
and mod_delayed_work() collapses a storm of teardowns into one run 3 s later.
A bus with no slow devices on it never walks anything.

The drop path is untouched - it fires from dwc2_hcd_qh_create() on the first
URB for an endpoint that would need a split, once per endpoint, never per
transfer.

Verified on the board. The repro above now produces zero extra switches, and
removal still upgrades: with both full-speed ports logically disabled the port
went to high speed, and dropped back when they returned.

    [   2.44] root port -> full speed   (full-speed devices present at boot)
    [ 154.89] root port -> high speed   (both ports disabled, tree clear)
    [ 156.28] root port -> full speed   (they came back)

--- a/drivers/usb/dwc2/hcd.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/dwc2/hcd.c
@@ -4895,6 +4895,57 @@
  * comes back at the new speed - 12 Mbit/s with FSLSSupp set, in which
  * case no device behind it needs a split any more.
  */
+/*
+ * Is a device that needs split transactions still attached?
+ *
+ * "An endpoint went away" is NOT the same question, and answering the wrong
+ * one cost four seconds of dead keyboard per event. _dwc2_hcd_endpoint_disable
+ * fires for every teardown - a driver unbinding, a device being reconfigured,
+ * an unrelated device being unplugged - so clearing split_devices there
+ * declared the bus clear while a full-speed keyboard was still sitting on it.
+ * The port then bounced to high speed, tore down every device on the bus, took
+ * the keyboard's first URB, found it needed a split again, and bounced back.
+ * Reproduced deliberately with "echo 0 > authorized" on the mouse: two extra
+ * speed switches and three disconnects, ~4 s with no input devices at all.
+ *
+ * So ask the bus instead of guessing. A device needs splits when it is slower
+ * than high speed AND sits behind a hub - parent->parent is the root hub, so a
+ * full-speed device plugged straight into the root port has no grandparent and
+ * needs no split, the port simply runs at its speed.
+ */
+struct dwc2_split_scan {
+	struct usb_bus *bus;
+	bool found;
+};
+
+static int dwc2_scan_one_device(struct usb_device *udev, void *data)
+{
+	struct dwc2_split_scan *scan = data;
+
+	if (udev->bus == scan->bus && udev->speed < USB_SPEED_HIGH &&
+	    udev->parent && udev->parent->parent)
+		scan->found = true;
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static bool dwc2_split_device_present(struct dwc2_hsotg *hsotg)
+{
+	struct dwc2_split_scan scan = {
+		.bus = &dwc2_hsotg_to_hcd(hsotg)->self,
+		.found = false,
+	};
+
+	/*
+	 * Walks the USB device tree under a mutex, so this may only be called
+	 * from the delayed work - process context, holding neither hsotg->lock
+	 * nor any device lock.
+	 */
+	usb_for_each_dev(&scan, dwc2_scan_one_device);
+
+	return scan.found;
+}
+
 static void dwc2_speed_switch_work(struct work_struct *work)
 {
 	struct dwc2_hsotg *hsotg = container_of(to_delayed_work(work),
@@ -4905,6 +4956,19 @@
 
 	if (hsotg->params.speed == speed)
 		return;
+
+	/*
+	 * Going back up is only safe once nothing on the bus needs a split.
+	 * Declining costs nothing: the port stays where it is and no device
+	 * is disturbed.
+	 */
+	if (speed != DWC2_SPEED_PARAM_FULL && dwc2_split_device_present(hsotg)) {
+		hsotg->split_devices = true;
+		dev_dbg(hsotg->dev,
+			"staying at full speed: a device behind the hub still needs splits\n");
+		return;
+	}
+
 	dev_info(hsotg->dev, "root port -> %s speed%s\n",
 		 speed == DWC2_SPEED_PARAM_FULL ? "full" : "high",
 		 speed == DWC2_SPEED_PARAM_FULL ?
@@ -4945,10 +5009,13 @@
 }
 
 /*
- * An endpoint went away. If the port is at full speed only because of a
- * device that needed splits, that device may be the one that left: try
- * high speed again, debounced. The switch itself tears every endpoint
- * down, so ignore the storm it causes.
+ * An endpoint went away. That device MAY have been the one that needed
+ * splits, so this is a hint to reconsider - not a statement that the bus is
+ * clear. dwc2_speed_switch_work re-checks the actual device tree before it
+ * touches the port, because acting on the hint alone flapped the port and
+ * killed input for seconds at a time.
+ *
+ * The switch itself tears every endpoint down, so ignore the storm it causes.
  */
 void dwc2_speed_note_endpoint_gone(struct dwc2_hsotg *hsotg)
 {
